#!/usr/bin/env etcs
#IMPORT ACE_ROOT/modules

# A WINDOW AND AN INPUT EDGE, AND NOTHING ELSE. The window is blank on purpose
# -- there is no renderer in this script at all, so nothing ever draws into it.
# That is the point: it is the smallest thing that proves the window and its
# event stream work, with no GPU anywhere in the picture.
#
#     ./bin/etcs scripts/graphical_script.etcs
#
# What you should see: an empty window that responds to keys (watch the log for
# KEY DOWN / KEY UP from ConsumeEvents) and closes on the cross.
#
# TO MAKE IT RENDER, add a GPU and a surface, then draw once -- the surface
# RETAINS its composition, so a single set of calls keeps being presented
# rather than blinking out on the next frame:
#
#     spawn RenderProvider::Instance gpu
#     gpu.Create()
#     main.spawn(RenderProvider::Surface view)
#     view.Create(@gpu)
#     view.Clear(0.05, 0.06, 0.10, 1.0)
#     view.DrawRect(80, 80, 240, 160, 0.85, 0.35, 0.30, 1.0)
#     detach render_frames.etcs view=view
#
# with the Create lines BEFORE main.Run (Run blocks for the window's lifetime),
# and the detach after them. render_script.etcs is that script, written out.
#
# For a scene that keeps changing rather than one still picture, bind a tree to
# the surface instead of drawing into it -- view.Compose(@root) makes the frame
# edge re-walk that tree every tick. composited_scenes.etcs and
# render_scene3d.etcs are the two worked examples.

spawn WindowProvider::Window main
detach WindowProvider/scripts/window_events.etcs window=main
main.Run(600, 600, 'GraphicalLoader Test GUI') 
main.Delete()
